As an edge case the result is true for $p = 2$; assume $p >= 3$. Apart from self-inverse elements, the units modulo $p$ come in pairs where each pair multiplies to $1$ modulo $p$; a self-inverse element $x$ satisfies $x^2 equiv 1 mod p$. By the lemma above, the only self-inverse elements are $1$ and $p-1$, so the remaining $p - 3$ units of $ZZ_p$ come in inverse pairs. Hence
$ (p-1)! &equiv 1 times (p-1) times ("pairs that multiply to" 1) \
         &equiv (p-1) \
         &equiv -1 mod p. $
